USER VISIBLE CHANGES BETWEEN TAO-4.0.8 and TAO-4.0.9
====================================================

. CORBA::ORB_init() throws CORBA::BAD_PARAM when the ORB core's init()
  fails by returning -1 (e.g. -ORBLaneEndpoint followed by another option
  instead of its endpoints). It ignored that result and returned a nil
  ORB without an exception, so the application crashed on its first use.
  The ORB core's init() now also runs inside ORB_init's cleanup, so the
  interceptors the initializers registered are destroyed when it fails.

. Notification Service: discarding by PriorityOrder no longer dereferences
  a null message block when the dequeue fails (empty or deactivated
  queue); found by the clang static analyzer.

. Data races found by ThreadSanitizer in multi-threaded clients and
  servers are fixed (each a real hazard on ARM and other weakly ordered
  CPUs):
  - TAO_Singleton and TAO_TSS_Singleton publish their instance through a
    std::atomic pointer, as the ACE singletons now do;
  - a transport's codeset translators, set from the codeset context of
    each request, and TAO_Stub's profile_success_ flag are atomic;
  - the lazily created allocators of TAO_Thread_Lane_Resources, the
    ORB core's RootPOA adapter, endpoint selector factory, ORB
    initializer registry and policy factory registry, and the resolved
    address of IIOP, SSLIOP, DIOP, SCIOP, SHMIOP and HTIOP endpoints are
    published atomically for their double-checked locking.
  The ORB initializer and policy factory registries are also looked up
  without holding the ORB core's lock, and ORBInitializer_Registry calls
  the initializers' pre_init () and post_init () without holding its own
  lock: both orders were the reverse of service initialization, which
  holds the service repository's lock while it registers initializers
  (potential deadlocks).

. CORBA::ORB_init() destroys the ORB it was creating when an ORB
  initializer's post_init() throws or a prod host refuses to start, instead
  of leaking it with the interceptors and managers the initializers had
  registered.

. SSLIOP transport hardening. In prod an SSLIOP service opens no plain
  IIOP listener: its references advertise IIOP port 0 and only the SSL
  port accepts connections. Every failed TLS handshake is an audit record
  with the peer's address and why it failed (NO_CLIENT_CERT,
  CERT_UNTRUSTED, CERT_EXPIRED, TLS_HANDSHAKE_FAILED). While the security
  state is degraded, new connections are refused (CONNECTION_REFUSED).

. SSLIOP checks a signed deny-list of certificate serial numbers and SPIFFE
  IDs (security.conf: denylist, denylist_signer, denylist_signer_sha256,
  denylist_reload, denylist_max_age). A listed certificate is refused at
  the handshake, and a request on an existing connection gets
  NO_PERMISSION once its identity is listed (CERT_DENYLISTED). The list is
  re-read when it changes; a list whose signature does not verify, or
  whose version is not newer, is refused and the last good one kept
  (DENYLIST_REJECTED). Prod requires the signer to be pinned by its
  SPKI SHA-256. The startup self-check reports the list's version and
  age. ace-tao-setup denylist add|remove|sign|check|pin maintains the list
  (DENYLIST_SIGNED).

. New SecurityLevel3 acquisition method "SL3TLSKeystore": credentials whose
  private key stays in a keystore (PKCS#11 token, TPM, Secure Enclave),
  named by an OpenSSL store URI, with the password used as the PIN. A
  software key that could be exported is refused. In prod, "SL3TLS" refuses
  an unencrypted PEM key.

. New test orbsvcs/tests/Security/Transport.

. Every request on an SSLIOP (TLS) connection is now decided on its caller.
  The server request interceptor builds a caller record from the peer
  certificate (SPIFFE IDs, RFC 2253 subject and issuer, serial, the peer's
  address) and asks TAO's AccessDecision through the new
  TAO::SL2::AccessDecision::access_allowed_caller(), passing the caller as
  SecurityLevel2 received credentials (TAO::Security::Caller_Credentials:
  AccessId and AuditId attributes) and the target as an object path
  ("/" + POA names + "/" + object id). Before, AccessDecision was consulted
  only for calls without TLS, with an empty credentials list. Calls without
  TLS are decided as before (add_object, default_decision). Code that
  implements TAO::SL2::AccessDecision itself must add the new operation and
  the default_caller_decision attribute.

. Servants read the caller through SecurityLevel3::SecurityCurrent:
  ClientCredentials::client_principal() now returns a SimplePrincipal named
  by the effective principal (the SPIFFE ID, else the subject DN; name type
  "TAO:SPIFFEID" or SL3 NT_X509DirectoryNamePath), with the record's other
  names as alternate names.

. SSLIOP decides identified callers with a signed access list
  (security.conf: access_list, access_list_signer, access_list_signer_sha256,
  access_list_reload, access_default, audit_grants). Rules allow or deny a
  principal (or * for any identified caller) an object path (exact, a
  prefix ending in *, or *) and optionally named operations (none, or *
  alone, means all); a deny wins.
  A request no rule covers gets access_default (allow unless set to deny);
  prod always refuses it and refuses to start without a pinned, loadable
  list. Refusals are audit records (ACCESS_DENIED, with the principal,
  subject, serial, peer, object and operation); grants too with
  audit_grants = on. The list is re-read when it changes; one that does
  not verify or is not newer is refused and the last good one kept
  (ACCESS_LIST_LOADED, ACCESS_LIST_REJECTED). ace-tao-setup access
  sign|check|test signs a hand-edited list (--bump sets the next version),
  verifies it and shows what it decides for one request
  (ACCESS_LIST_SIGNED). New test orbsvcs/tests/Security/Caller.

. SSLIOP references carry the CSIv2 TLS component. With security.conf
  csiv2 = supported or required (the prod default; off elsewhere), every
  SSLIOP profile gets a TAG_CSI_SEC_MECH_LIST component beside
  TAG_SSL_SEC_TRANS: one compound mechanism whose transport is
  TAG_TLS_SEC_TRANS, listing the TLS endpoint's host and port and its
  association options (EstablishTrustInClient when client certificates are
  verified or required). No authentication or attribute layer is
  advertised yet. A TAO client takes the TLS port from TAG_TLS_SEC_TRANS
  when a reference has no TAG_SSL_SEC_TRANS (a CSIv2-only server such as
  JacORB), and refuses a profile whose two components name different TLS
  ports (IOR_TLS_MISMATCH). New test orbsvcs/tests/Security/CSIv2_IOR.

. SSLIOP servers implement the CSIv2 Security Attribute Service (SAS),
  stateless, over TLS. A request's SAS service context is decoded:
  EstablishContext with a GSSUP client authentication token is checked
  against the GSSUP target name (security.conf gssup_target) and the
  installed TAO::Security::GSSUP_Verifier, and answered with
  CompleteEstablishContext (context_stateful false); the user (user@realm)
  becomes the request's effective principal for the access decision and
  for the servant. Everything else gets a ContextError and NO_PERMISSION
  per CORBA 3.3 Part 2 10.3.5: a wrong password, unknown user or wrong
  target major 1 (with the GSSUP error UNSPECIFIED, so they are
  indistinguishable), a MessageInContext major 4 (no contexts are kept),
  identity assertion or another mechanism major 2. Authorization tokens are
  ignored. csiv2 = required, or csiv2_gssup = required, refuses a request
  without a SAS context. With csiv2_gssup = supported or required the
  references advertise GSSUP in their authentication layer. Audit reasons
  SAS_ACCEPTED (with audit_grants), SAS_MISSING, SAS_INVALID_EVIDENCE,
  SAS_INVALID_MECH, SAS_NO_CONTEXT, SAS_NO_TLS, SAS_AUTHZ_TOKEN_IGNORED,
  GSSUP_FAILED; passwords are never logged. Prod refuses csiv2_gssup without
  a target name or a verifier. Tokens framed with JacORB's non-DER length
  are accepted. New test orbsvcs/tests/Security/SAS.

. GSSUP password store. With security.conf gssup_store set, SSLIOP checks
  GSSUP logins against a local file of scrypt hashes (OpenSSL 3 EVP_KDF),
  maintained with "ace-tao-setup user add|passwd|lock|unlock|remove|list"
  (passwords from the terminal or --password-stdin, never the command line;
  at least 12 characters; the file is rewritten atomically with mode 0600).
  The store is re-read when it changes. It is unusable, and every login is
  refused, when it cannot be parsed or, in prod, when it is readable by
  others or not owned by the service; the startup self-check reports it
  (gssup_store). Guessing limits: gssup_max_failures failures (10) within
  gssup_failure_window seconds (900) lock a user for gssup_lockout seconds
  (1800); gssup_source_max_failures (50) do the same for a host. A locked
  user, an unknown user and a wrong password get the same answer and cost
  the same scrypt. Audit reasons GSSUP_LOCKED, GSSUP_RATE_LIMITED,
  GSSUP_STORE_LOADED, GSSUP_STORE_REJECTED, GSSUP_USER_CHANGED.
  TAO::Security::GSSUP_Verifier::verify now takes the caller's source
  address and returns why a login failed. New test
  orbsvcs/tests/Security/GSSUP_Store.

. CSIv2 identity assertion on SSLIOP servers. With security.conf
  csiv2_asserters naming a signed asserter list (same signer and pinning
  as the deny-list; "ace-tao-setup asserters sign|check"), an identity
  token is accepted only from a listed asserter, matched on its
  certificate (SPIFFE ID or subject) or on its own GSSUP login, and only
  for the identity types (name, dn, x509, anonymous) and realms its entry
  allows (default: names in the service's own realm). Asserted
  user@realm names, distinguished names and certificate chains (verified
  against the trust store and the deny-list) become the caller's
  effective principal; the asserter stays in the caller record, in the
  servant's alternate names, in the audit records (SAS_ACCEPTED,
  ACCESS_GRANTED/DENIED carry asserter=) and in access-list rules, which
  can require it with via=ASSERTER. Refusals are ContextError 1
  (ASSERTION_REJECTED), or 2 for an unsupported token type or with no
  asserter list. allow_anonymous_assertion = on accepts an anonymous
  assertion from any caller (CSIv2 rule 4); = listed only from listed
  asserters allowed "anonymous"; the default is off (self-check warns on
  either). "asserter *" (presumed trust) is refused. References
  advertise identity assertion in their SAS layer. New test
  orbsvcs/tests/Security/Assertion.

. CSIv2 SAS on the TAO client. SSLIOP now sends a stateless
  EstablishContext to targets whose CSIv2 component asks for one, and
  turns a ContextError reply into NO_PERMISSION (audited as
  SAS_CLIENT_REFUSED). GSSUP credentials are acquired through
  SecurityLevel3 with the new "SL3GSSUP" method and an
  SSLIOP::GSSUPAuthData argument: a user@realm and a password source
  ("prompt", "fd:N" or "file:PATH", the file owner-only), never the
  password itself. They are sent only over TLS and only to a target
  whose GSSUP target name matches theirs; a target that requires GSSUP
  without matching credentials is refused locally
  (SAS_CLIENT_NO_CREDENTIALS). With csiv2_assert_caller = on, calls a
  servant makes assert the caller of the request it is serving (its
  user@realm, or its certificate chain) to targets that support identity
  assertion. A ContextEstablishmentPolicy can carry the credentials or
  turn client authentication off. The SSLIOP connector now takes the
  first TLS credential of such a policy's list rather than only the
  first entry. New test orbsvcs/tests/Security/SAS_Client.

. The full role map (Phase 3 T3). A role line names a principal (a
  subject DN may now hold blanks), group:NAME, sid:SID or anonymous, and
  may carry via=ASSERTER (only when that asserter asserted the caller) or
  factors=tls+gssup (only when the caller itself proved a certificate and
  a GSSUP password). group:NAME matches members listed by "group NAME
  PRINCIPAL" lines, or a GSSUP login whose store line has NAME in its new
  groups= field (ace-tao-setup user groups USER --set G,... | --clear);
  sid:SID matches an AD group SID from a verified Kerberos ticket, and
  "sid SID NAME" gives it a readable name for audit records. Ticket and
  store groups belong to the login on the request, never to a caller it
  asserts. A caller with no identity gets roles only from an "anonymous"
  line. An asserter-list entry may cap the roles its asserted callers can
  reach with roles=. Authz audit records add roles_from and
  roles_capped; SAS_ACCEPTED adds login_groups. GSSUP_Verifier has a new
  virtual groups (user), empty by default. ace-tao-setup roles test
  takes --cert, --groups, --sids, --asserted/--via/--cap and --anonymous,
  and the new ace-tao-setup roles import drafts a role map from an
  interim access list.

. Role-based access decisions (Phase 3 T2). With roles_dir set, SSLIOP
  decides each request from the three signed tables instead of the
  interim access list: the rights table names the Security Service
  rights (get, set, manage, use; all or any) each operation of an
  interface requires, matched on the target's most-derived interface,
  with "inherit DERIVED BASE" lines so a derived interface uses its bases'
  lines; the grants table gives each role its rights for the whole
  service, or for a POA subtree after a "domain" line; the roles table
  gives principals their roles. In prod an operation no line covers is
  refused; CORBA's built-in operations need get unless listed. An access
  list, if configured alongside, still applies its explicit deny rules
  first. Each authz audit record names the interface, the roles held, the
  rights required, the domain and the table versions. SecurityLevel2
  RequiredRights answers from the rights table; set_required_rights is
  refused in prod and applied in memory only in test mode
  (REQUIRED_RIGHTS_SET, REQUIRED_RIGHTS_REFUSED). Table bodies are syntax
  checked by the loader and by ace-tao-setup ... sign, and a new
  ace-tao-setup roles test gives the decision for a principal, interface,
  operation and object offline. Recommended tables for the Naming and
  Notification Services ship as unsigned drafts in
  orbsvcs/Security_Setup/tables. Groups and conditions in the role map
  come in T3.

. Signed policy tables for the role model (Phase 3 T1). A new loader,
  TAO::SSLIOP::Policy_Tables, reads the required-rights, grants and
  role-map tables from roles_dir: dated files (KIND-YYYY-MM-DD.txt, or
  .N.txt for a further revision that day) beside detached signatures,
  each starting with signed table, version and issued lines. The winner
  for each table is the highest version among files that verify with the
  pinned roles signer (roles_signer, roles_signer_sha256), name their own
  table, and carry a signed issued date equal to the one in their name, so
  an older table renamed to look newer is refused. With roles_state, the
  highest version accepted is recorded and a lower one is refused after a
  restart. Files are re-read every roles_reload seconds (30); a failing
  file leaves the last good table in force and is audited once
  (ROLES_TABLE_LOADED, ROLES_TABLE_REJECTED). With roles_dir set the
  self-check needs all three tables and a roles signer that is not the
  deny-list signer, and fails prod otherwise. New ace-tao-setup commands
  rights|grants|roles sign (next version, dated name, signature written
  first) and check (which file a service would load, and why the others
  are refused). The tables' contents and the decisions they drive come
  in T2 and T3. New test orbsvcs/tests/Security/Roles.

. SSLIOP with OpenSSL 3: -SSLEcName sets the key-exchange groups with
  SSL_CTX_set1_groups_list () (OpenSSL 1.1.1 and later) instead of the
  deprecated EC_KEY and SSL_CTX_set_tmp_ecdh (), which also leaked the
  EC_KEY; it still limits TLS to the named curve, and now takes a
  colon-separated list (e.g. X25519:P-384). OpenSSL_traits<EVP_PKEY>::copy ()
  duplicates any key type with EVP_PKEY_dup () (OpenSSL 3), or shares it
  (OpenSSL 1.1); before, EC and EdDSA keys gave a null copy, a DSA copy lost
  its private key and an RSA copy leaked. Two missing override specifiers
  added (SSLIOP_Transport.h, SSLIOP_Profile.h).

. Kerberos names in CSIv2 identity assertions (Phase 3 K5). Where
  csiv2_kerberos is on, references list the Kerberos mechanism among the
  supported naming mechanisms, and a listed asserter may assert a Kerberos
  principal (an exported name of the Kerberos mechanism) in a realm it is
  allowed: realms=@REALM or *, by default the service principal's own
  realm. Malformed names, other realms and unknown mechanism OIDs are
  refused with ContextError 1 and audited. TAO's middle tier asserts a
  Kerberos-authenticated caller as a Kerberos name, only to targets that
  list Kerberos naming, never re-labelled as GSSUP. Caller records each
  name's mechanism (authenticated_mech, asserted_mech), and SecurityLevel3
  reports Kerberos principals with NT_KerberosName.

. AD group SIDs from Kerberos tickets (Phase 3 K4). When an accepted ticket
  carries an Active Directory PAC that the GSS-API library verified with
  the ticket, its logon information (MS-PAC KERB_VALIDATION_INFO) gives
  the user SID and group SIDs: domain groups, extra SIDs and resource
  groups, without deny-only ones; no directory is asked. They go on the
  caller (TAO::Security::Caller::groups, for the authenticated principal
  only, never an asserted one), into SAS_ACCEPTED audit records (user_sid,
  groups) and into the SecurityLevel3 principal as privileges (authority
  TAO:KerberosRealm, type TAO:ADGroupSID). An unverified or unreadable PAC
  grants no group. The NDR parser, TAO::SSLIOP::Kerberos::
  parse_logon_info (), is bounds-checked, caps lists at 4096 groups and
  needs no GSS-API; the Kerberos test checks it against a real AD PAC.

. CSIv2 Kerberos logins from TAO clients (Phase 3 K3; needs the gssapi
  build). The new SecurityLevel3 acquisition method "SL3Kerberos" takes an
  SSLIOP::KerberosAuthData: a ticket cache (default KRB5CCNAME, as left by
  kinit) or, for a service, a client keytab and principal, whose TGT goes
  to a private in-memory cache; and the target names the credentials may
  log in to (a service principal, or @REALM; default the client's realm).
  No password passes through TAO. TAO's SAS client prefers a Kerberos
  mechanism it has credentials for over GSSUP, sends the token only over
  TLS, bound to the connection (the server certificate's RFC 5929 hash),
  and asks for mutual authentication: a reply without the service's valid
  Kerberos reply token raises NO_PERMISSION (COMPLETED_MAYBE) and is
  audited as KRB_SERVER_UNVERIFIED. A target that requires Kerberos, with
  no usable credentials, is refused locally. TAO-specific accessors
  TAO_ClientRequestInfo::tao_transport () and tao_invocation () let client
  interceptors reach the request's connection.

. CSIv2 Kerberos logins on the TAO target (Phase 3 K2; needs the gssapi
  build). csiv2_kerberos = off | supported | required, with krb_principal
  (the service principal), krb_keytab and optionally krb_rcache: a client
  authentication token of the Kerberos V5 mechanism is accepted with the
  keytab, the caller becomes user@REALM and the Kerberos reply goes back in
  CompleteEstablishContext. By default the token must be bound to the TLS
  session with GSS channel bindings carrying the target certificate's hash
  (RFC 5929 tls-server-end-point; krb_channel_binding = required |
  supported | off). Unbound, wrongly bound, replayed, expired and
  wrong-service tokens are refused with ContextError 1 and audited as
  KRB_FAILED with the reason. References list one compound mechanism per
  login mechanism, Kerberos first, then GSSUP; a required mechanism is
  offered alone and refuses the other. TAO's client now picks the first
  mechanism it can use. The self-check refuses a keytab without keys for
  the principal, with DES or RC4 keys, or (in prod) not owner-only.
  Kerberos clock skew follows krb5.conf clockskew (default 300 s).

. SSLIOP can be built with GSS-API (MIT Kerberos) for CSIv2 Kerberos logins,
  the groundwork for Phase 3: the new MPC feature gssapi (off by default;
  base project ace_gssapi, GSSAPI_ROOT or GSSAPI_INCDIR/GSSAPI_LIBDIR)
  links libgssapi_krb5 and defines TAO_HAS_GSSAPI. TAO::SSLIOP::Kerberos
  reports whether it was built, whether the library offers the Kerberos V5
  mechanism, and the library's text for GSS status codes; its header names
  no GSS type. Windows (SSPI) is not supported yet. New test
  orbsvcs/tests/Security/Kerberos.

. GSSUP usernames are CSIv2 scoped-usernames (CORBA 3.3 Part 2,
  10.2.5.2) on both sides: a target accepts "alice" or "alice@lab.test"
  (a scope other than its gssup_target is refused), with '@' and '\'
  inside a name quoted by '\'. Asserted GSSUP names are parsed the same
  way.

. SSLIOP never negotiates below TLS 1.2: SSL 3.0, TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1 are
  refused in every mode, whatever -SSLVersionList says (asking for them
  logs a warning). The startup self-check reports it (tls_versions).

. ace-tao-setup makes service keys and gets their certificates. "key create"
  generates a key where it will stay: in a PKCS#11 token (pkcs11-provider),
  a TPM (tpm2-openssl, as a TPM-wrapped blob), the macOS Secure Enclave (the
  sep provider's sep-keygen), or as a passphrase-encrypted PKCS#8 file
  (unprotected files only with --unprotected, never in prod). "key check"
  loads a key as SSLIOP would, reports whether its private half can be
  exported, makes a test signature and matches it against a certificate.
  "cert request" turns a step-ca one-time token into a certificate: the
  request carries exactly the token's subject and SANs, is signed where the
  key lives, and the root certificate must match the token's fingerprint.
  "cert renew" renews over mutual TLS with the current certificate and key
  once less than a third of its lifetime is left. Key creation, issue and
  renewal are audit records (KEY_CREATED, CERT_ISSUED, CERT_RENEWED).

. Certificate expiry and authenticated time are checked. SSLIOP's startup
  self-check (so every service, ace-tao-setup check --svc-conf and the
  hourly check) reports the service certificate's remaining validity: an
  expired or not-yet-valid certificate fails prod, and one that expires
  within cert_warn_days (default 14) warns in every mode. ace-tao-setup
  check reports whether chrony authenticates its time sources with NTS
  (RFC 8915), since plain NTP can be spoofed to make an expired
  certificate look valid; missing NTS warns, and require_nts = yes makes it
  fail prod. ace-tao-setup install and uninstall also gained accurate dry
  runs and a note before the macOS account steps.

. New tool ace-tao-setup (orbsvcs/Security_Setup) prepares and checks a
  host's security configuration: status; check [--svc-conf FILE] [--json]
  (host checks, plus a service's own startup self-check run in-process
  without opening a port; exit 0 OK, 1 warnings, 2 failures, for systemd
  ExecStartPre, timers and monitoring); init --mode MODE
  [--trust-anchor-file PEM] (writes security.conf atomically, showing the
  CA fingerprints for confirmation); set-mode; enroll (writes the prod
  enrollment marker after the host checks pass); unenroll --reason TEXT.
  Every change is written to the audit log; leaving prod needs
  confirmation, and every mode but prod is refused on an enrolled host.
  The audit logger now drops caller fields named like its own (mode, svc,
  event, result, reason). New test orbsvcs/tests/Security/Setup.

. SSLIOP no longer closes a -SSLPassword file: passphrase file twice. The
  ACE_Read_Buffer reading it closed the file on destruction after the code
  had already closed it; glibc aborts on that (double free), so on Linux a
  passphrase-protected key with -SSLPassword file: could crash the process
  at startup. Found by the new orbsvcs/tests/Security/Mode test on Linux.

. Host security modes. A host opts in with a security configuration file,
  ACE_TAO_SECURITY_CONF or /etc/ace-tao/security.conf ("key = value"
  lines), whose mode applies to every TAO process: dev (developer machines,
  synthetic data; no self-check, plugin optional), test (QA or staging
  mirroring prod; the self-check runs and warns), prod (live data;
  enforced) or embedded (a declared constrained device that cannot run the
  security plugin; runs without it, with a loud banner). dev and embedded
  announce themselves once per process. Without the file nothing changes. With it, CORBA::ORB_init (new
  tao/Security_Mode.h, in libTAO so that removing a plugin cannot remove
  the check) refuses to run, before any port is opened, when the file has
  no valid mode, is writable by others, or names any mode but prod (or is
  missing) on a host enrolled in prod (/var/lib/ace-tao/enrolled-prod); and in prod
  unless the security plugin reports that its self-check passed. The
  self-check (Security library, run when SSLIOP loads it) checks the audit
  sink, clock synchronization (ntp_adjtime; prod waits up to
  time_sync_wait, default 300 s, within max_clock_error, default 1000 ms),
  turns core dumps off, and requires that only TLS protocol factories are
  loaded (allowed_protocols, default SSLIOP_Factory). SSLIOP adds: the
  private key must be a keystore key or a passphrase-protected file, the
  passphrase must come from file: or prompt:, -SSLNoProtection is refused,
  and the -SSLCAfile certificates must all be listed in
  trust_anchor_sha256. In test mode the same findings are warnings and a
  TEST MODE reminder is logged hourly. A banner with the mode, key type,
  trust anchor, time and audit state and the configuration's SHA-256 goes
  to stderr, syslog and the audit log. While a prod service runs, requests
  are refused with CORBA::TRANSIENT while the audit sink is down, and lost
  time synchronization raises TIME_UNSYNCED alarms without stopping the
  service. The Notification Service refuses event persistence in prod
  (topology persistence is unaffected). New test
  orbsvcs/tests/Security/Mode.

. The Security library has an audit logger, and SecurityLevel2's
  SecurityManager::audit_decision() returns a working AuditDecision instead
  of raising CORBA::NO_IMPLEMENT. Its AuditChannel, and the C++ class
  TAO::Security::Audit_Logger, write one syslog record per security decision
  with facility authpriv, as RFC 5424 structured data:
    [audit@32473 mode="prod" svc="naming" event="authz" result="deny"
     reason="NO_ROLE" src="10.20.4.17:51544" op="bind"]
  Sinks: syslog(3) (default on macOS), unix:PATH (default unix:/dev/log),
  tcp:HOST:PORT (RFC 5424 with RFC 6587 framing, e.g. to syslog-ng) and
  stderr. Repeated failures from one source are rate limited into a summary
  record. If the sink fails, records go to stderr, a SECURITY_DEGRADED alarm
  is raised (stderr, syslog(3) and registered callbacks) and the sink is
  retried; the recovery is itself recorded. Settings come from the host file
  named by ACE_TAO_SECURITY_CONF (default /etc/ace-tao/security.conf):
  mode, audit_sink and audit_rate (COUNT/SECONDS, default 20/60); the svc
  field from ACE_TAO_AUDIT_SERVICE or the program name. New test
  orbsvcs/tests/Security/Audit.

. SSLIOP's -SSLPrivateKey accepts a keystore key: STORE:<uri> with an OpenSSL 3
  store URI, or a PKCS#11 URI as is (pkcs11:token=...;object=...). The PIN is
  given with -SSLPassword. -SSLCertificate still takes a file.

. A corbaloc or corbaname URL with several addresses and no object key (the
  default "NameService"), such as corbaloc::host1:2809,:host2:2809, no longer
  crashes the ORB. The address scanner compared the position of the next ','
  with a null '/' position and computed a garbage length. Fixed for IIOP
  (TAO_Connector) and SSLIOP; new test tests/CORBALOC_Default_Key.

. TAO skeleton argument storage for basic and special-basic argument, return,
  and out values is now value-initialized, preventing indeterminate scalar
  values from reaching marshaling code.

. Generated union discriminator setters now throw CORBA::BAD_PARAM when a new
  discriminator would select a different union member. Discriminator values
  selecting the currently active member remain accepted.

. SSLIOP now loads every certificate in a PEM -SSLCertificate file, so a
  certificate followed by its intermediate CA certificates is sent to peers as
  a full chain. Previously only the first certificate was used, and peers that
  trusted just the root CA failed the handshake.

. SecurityLevel3 credential acquisition works: CredentialsCurator kept the
  acquisition methods in an ACE_Map_Manager keyed on const char *, which
  compares pointers, so acquire_credentials ("SL3TLS", ...) always raised
  CORBA::BAD_PARAM. SSLIOP credentials acquired from a PEM certificate file
  now also carry the intermediate CA certificates that follow the first one,
  and an invocation using them sends exactly that chain instead of the one
  belonging to the default certificate.

. The Notification Service now drops queued events whose Timeout has passed
  instead of delivering them late. The deadline comes from the event's own
  Timeout header, or else from the Timeout QoS of its proxy, admin or channel,
  counted from when the channel received the event. Expired events also give
  up their place before MaxEventsPerConsumer or MaxQueueLength limits apply to
  live ones. Previously Timeout only influenced DeadlineOrder queues, and on
  platforms without ACE_HAS_TIMED_MESSAGE_BLOCKS (all but INTEGRITY) not even
  that. Behavior change: suppliers that put small Timeout values in event
  headers only to sort events will now lose those events once the Timeout
  passes; give each event a Timeout that is a real lifetime.

. The Notification Service's DeadlineOrder OrderPolicy and DiscardPolicy now
  work without ACE_HAS_TIMED_MESSAGE_BLOCKS. Its queues order and pick events
  by the deadline Notify stores for each event instead of the ACE message
  block deadline, which is only kept on INTEGRITY; elsewhere DeadlineOrder
  behaved like FIFO. The Ordering and Discarding tests no longer refuse
  their deadline modes, and a new unit test, tests/unit/Notify/Deadline_Queue,
  checks the queue directly.

. Notification Service topology persistence now survives a normal shutdown.
  On shutdown the service destroyed every channel, and each destroy was saved,
  so the topology file was empty and the next start had nothing to restore;
  only a crash kept it. With a Topology_Factory configured, channels are now
  shut down instead of destroyed, and nothing is saved once the factory has
  shut down.

USER VISIBLE CHANGES BETWEEN TAO-4.0.7 and TAO-4.0.8
